<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Sinus Pressure: Best Points" description="Use acupressure for sinus pressure and congestion. Guide to LI20, Yintang, LI4, GV20, and other points that ease facial congestion and stuffiness." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Sinus Pressure", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-sinus" }]} datePublished="2026-08-27" dateModified="2026-08-27" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Sinus Pressure: Best Points for Congestion Relief</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Sinus pressure from colds, allergies, or changes in air pressure can leave the face feeling tight and stuffy. A handful of points around the nose, brow, and skull base are traditionally used to encourage drainage and relieve that congested feeling. The routine below is a gentle self-care option to use alongside hydration, steam, and any treatment your clinician recommends.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>Research Note:</strong> Most support for sinus acupressure comes from traditional use and small studies rather than large trials. It may ease the sensation of pressure and support comfort, but it does not replace decongestants, antihistamines, or antibiotics when a bacterial sinus infection is present.</p></div><section id="li20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li20-yingxiang-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">1. LI20 (Yingxiang) - Welcome Fragrance</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LI20 sits right beside the nostrils and is the primary point for nasal congestion and stuffiness in traditional practice.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the nasolabial groove, beside the base of the nose on each side. <a href="/points/li20-yingxiang-acupressure-point">LI20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press upward along the side of the nose with your index fingers for 1-2 minutes, breathing through your nose if you can.</p></section><section id="yintang" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">2. Yintang - Hall of Impression</a></h2><p class="mb-3">Yintang, between the eyebrows, is traditionally used to open the nasal passages and calm pressure across the brow and sinuses.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> Midway between the eyebrows, at the bridge of the nose. <a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point">Yintang guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press gently with one fingertip in small circles for 1-2 minutes.</p></section><section id="li4" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">3. LI4 (Hegu) - Joining Valley</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LI4 on the hand is a broad pain and congestion point often paired with the local facial points for sinus discomfort.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the webbing between the thumb and index finger. <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point">LI4 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Squeeze the webbing firmly for 1-2 minutes per hand.</p></section><section id="gv20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">4. GV20 (Baihui) - Hundred Meetings</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GV20 at the crown is traditionally used to clear the head and support a sense of openness when the face feels congested.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the midline of the head, about two finger-widths behind the hairline at the top of the skull. <a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point">GV20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Gentle steady pressure with the fingertips for 1-2 minutes.</p></section><section id="gb20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">5. GB20 (Fengchi) - Wind Pool</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GB20 at the base of the skull is traditionally used to relieve pressure that travels from the sinuses into the head and neck.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the hollows at the base of the skull, between the two vertical neck muscles. <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point">GB20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press upward into the skull-base depressions with both thumbs for 1-2 minutes.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4"><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How often can I do this?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The facial points are gentle enough to repeat several times a day. Pair the routine with steam inhalation and plenty of fluids for the best comfort.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">See a clinician for sinus symptoms that last more than 10 days, high fever, thick green-yellow discharge with facial swelling or severe pain, or vision changes. These can signal a bacterial infection that needs medical treatment.</p></details></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Consult a licensed healthcare provider for persistent or severe sinus symptoms.</p></div></article>
